The correction had been running for almost an hour when B arrived.

The contract still described it as preliminary.

B walked beneath the covered entrance of the Continuity tower. Rain had not started yet, but Meridian had already dimmed itself in anticipation. Someone had requested him by credential through a private channel and declined to say who they were, and Mote had declined to elaborate, and B had accepted anyway because there was a live correction running without an operator and a person inside it running out of time.

He was still deciding whether that sequence of facts was reasonable.

A transit announcement followed pedestrians along the pavement.

APPROACHING LICENSED CONTINUITY SERVICES.

RELIEF IS A RESPONSIBLE CHOICE.

The clinic occupied the upper floors of a residential tower whose windows had been instructed to soften the city. Outside, Meridian was gray with approaching rain. Inside, the light remained warm and steady.

The elevator spoke as it carried him upward.

"Relief is a responsible choice."

B looked at the ceiling.

"You already said that."

"The elevator has detected emotional resistance."

"I'm tired."

"That is not one of its available interpretations."

The elevator continued upward.

B watched the floor numbers change.

The stimulant had settled into something less useful. His jaw hurt. The skin behind his ears felt too small for his skull.

The second capsule was gone.

He had disposed of it.

The doors opened onto a private Continuity floor.

Two people waited outside a sealed treatment room.

One wore clinical white. The other was younger, perhaps forty, dressed in soft dark clothes that looked selected by someone else. His posture was careful and exhausted, as though he had spent the day trying not to become another problem.

A small credential glowed near his wrist.

SIMON KADE
CONTINUITY COMMUNICATIONS

The clinician stepped forward.

"You're the replacement operator?"

"Yes."

"I'm Doctor Venn."

Simon looked at him for a moment before offering his hand.

"Simon Kade. The client's son."

B shook it.

"How long since the first operator withdrew?"

"Thirty-one minutes," Venn said.

"The contract said nineteen."

"It was nineteen when the request went out."

B glanced at Simon.

"Did you request me?"

Simon's hand returned to his side.

"The first operator left."

"That explains why you needed someone."

"My father is running out of time."

It was not an answer.

B looked at the sealed door.

"What happened?"

"The situation changed," Venn said.

"How?"

Venn glanced at Simon.

Simon said, "My father is inside."

"I know."

"He's been unstable for months."

Venn touched the door panel but did not open it.

"Mr. Kade has experienced severe insomnia, dissociation, aggression toward staff, and repeated attempts to leave secure environments."

"Why were the environments secure?"

"He was attempting to locate someone."

"Who?"

"That material is protected."

"The subject file is mostly redaction."

"The omitted material is not required for the authorized procedure."

B looked at the door again.

Behind it, the correction field pressed faintly against the clinic walls. Without a Chrysanthemum connection, it registered only as pressure behind his eyes.

"Why did the first Dev leave?"

Venn's expression changed very little.

"They identified an ethical concern."

"What concern?"

"They did not document it before withdrawing."

"So you called someone else."

"The subject cannot remain stabilized indefinitely."

Simon moved closer.

"My father consented."

B looked at him.

"I'm going to read what he consented to."

"He needs help."

"I know."

The answer came more gently than B intended.

Simon looked away.

Mote spoke privately through the patch.

"Subject coherence is declining."

"How long?"

"Withdrawal becomes unsafe in approximately thirty-eight minutes."

B looked at Venn.

"Open it."

The suite had been designed to resemble a bedroom.

It had succeeded in the way institutions succeeded at such things. There was a bed, soft lighting, wood-toned surfaces, and no object in the room that could not be cleaned, monitored, replaced, or used as evidence of calm.

A correction ring hung above the bed.

Pale articulated segments descended from the ceiling around a portable Chrysanthemum. Wet-looking membranes opened and closed along the ring's inner edge. Blue filaments pulsed beneath translucent composite. Optical nerves ran from the ring into the unit beside the wall.

Elias Kade lay beneath it.

He was seventy-three. Silver hair combed back from his forehead. One hand rested above the blanket, its fingers opening and closing by fractions.

The correction had already begun smoothing the small tensions through which age announced itself. His face looked rested, but not peacefully. The expression belonged to sedation, not sleep.

Six personal objects had been arranged near the bed as continuity anchors.

A watch.

A ceramic bird.

A folded napkin.

A book with no title visible.

A family photograph turned facedown.

A small wooden train with one metal wheel.

B stopped beside the train.

"Why this?"

Simon remained near the door.

"He made it."

"For you?"

"For us. My brother and me."

B picked it up.

The wood was dark and smooth from years of handling. Three wheels had been carved from the same material. The fourth was a metal button fixed through its center with a copper pin.

"What happened to the wheel?"

"It broke."

"He repaired it with a button."

"It was what he had."

B turned the train over.

Something had been written beneath it.

The ink remained, but the marks refused to settle into letters. Each time B began assembling them into a name, the correction field loosened them again.

"Who else used this?"

Simon's jaw tightened.

"No one relevant."

B looked at him.

Venn stepped closer.

"The authorized target is acute bereavement dysregulation associated with a deceased dependent."

"Name?"

"Redacted."

"Cause of death?"

"Not required for attenuation."

"When?"

"Outside the scope of the procedure."

B placed the train back down.

"Is the person dead?"

Neither of them answered.

Mote spoke into B's ear.

"One identity matching the concealed dependency remains active in Meridian."

B looked at Simon.

"Your sister used transit this morning."

Simon's face emptied.

Venn said, "The external identity is outside the recognized family domain."

"So she's alive."

"Legally," Venn said.

B waited.

Nobody added anything.

Simon came farther into the room and let the door close behind him.

"My sister left the family twelve years ago."

"That isn't the same as dying."

"You don't know what happened."

"No."

"She accessed internal audit records. There were environmental failures connected to a water-treatment contract. If the records had reached the regulator during renewal, the company would have gone into receivership."

"And?"

"Three hundred employees. Pensions. Contractors. People who had nothing to do with it."

"What happened to the records?"

Simon looked at the floor.

"The disclosure was contained."

B glanced at Elias.

His fingers kept opening and closing above the blanket.

"And your sister?"

"She kept her independent identity. Her work. Her home. Her public records."

"Just not the family."

Simon's mouth tightened.

"She chose what she chose."

B looked at Elias.

The train sat between them, one repaired wheel carrying more history than the room was willing to admit.

For a moment, nobody spoke.

The correction specification opened across the diagnostic glass.

TARGET RELATIONSHIP:

BEREAVEMENT RESPONSE / DECEASED DEPENDENT

AUTHORIZED CHANGES:

EMOTIONAL INTENSITY
RECURRING DREAM CONTENT
COMPULSIVE SEARCH BEHAVIOR
AGGRESSIVE RECOGNITION EVENTS
PATERNAL ATTACHMENT REINFORCEMENT

PROTECTED INVARIANTS:

SELF-RECOGNITION
LANGUAGE
EXECUTIVE FUNCTION
LEGAL COMPETENCE
RECOGNIZED FAMILY STRUCTURE
CORPORATE AUTHORITY
PUBLIC BIOGRAPHICAL CONTINUITY

B read it twice.

"Recognized family structure," he said.

"Yes," Venn replied.

"Meaning the family the records currently recognize."

"The authorized family."

"And the paternal attachment?"

"It is producing the dysregulation."

B looked at Elias.

"It may also be producing the father."

Simon's expression hardened.

"My father hasn't slept more than two hours at a time in six weeks. He wakes up calling her name. Last week he hit a nurse because she told him he had two children."

B looked at him.

"How many does he think he has?"

Simon did not answer.

B touched the diagnostic glass.

"Show me his consent."

The recording opened.

Elias sat in another room, unshaven and trembling. A clinician explained that the procedure would reduce emotional distress without removing factual memory.

"Play the end," B said.

Venn remained still.

"Mote."

The recording advanced.

Elias looked toward someone outside the frame.

"I don't want to forget her."

"The procedure is not designed to remove factual memory," the clinician replied.

"I want the days to stop repeating."

"That is the intended outcome."

"I want to sleep."

"We can help with that."

Someone placed a stylus in Elias's hand.

He signed.

The recording ended.

B looked at Venn.

"He agreed to emotional attenuation."

"Yes."

"This procedure is separating the attachment that keeps the memory connected to him."

"The relationship cannot be cleanly separated from the symptoms."

"But you told him he would remember."

"He may retain factual content."

"Without recognition."

Venn took a breath.

"I had a patient who refused attenuation," she said. "He lost his son in a correction accident. He said the grief was the last honest thing between them."

B waited.

"He kept it for four years. It didn't become meaningful. It didn't make him kinder. It took everything else."

She looked at Elias.

"I believe this treatment saves people."

B did not argue.

He believed her.

That was part of the problem.

Simon looked between them.

"Can you finish it?"

B looked at Elias.

The old man's mouth moved.

No sound emerged.

B leaned closer.

Elias opened his eyes.

They were pale beneath the correction light.

"Leah," he whispered.

His fingers stopped moving.

The correction ring continued its soft mechanical pulse above him.

Simon shut his eyes.

He remained that way for several seconds.

When he opened them again, something in his face had been put back behind a door.

B pulled the work chair toward the portable Chrysanthemum.

"Doctor Venn, I need full dependency visibility."

"That exceeds your contract."

"I need to see what the procedure is separating."

Mote spoke privately.

"I can hold clinical control for ninety seconds."

Venn looked toward the nearest speaker.

"You are not authorized to do that."

"No," Mote said.

B fitted the contact band around the base of his skull.

Mote said, "This is your decision."

"I know."

The portable Chrysanthemum opened around him.

The suite remained visible.

Then Elias Kade's continuity field unfolded through it.

B did not see a sequence of memories.

He felt a house arranged around a missing person.

A bedroom kept cooler because someone slept badly in heat.

A dining table organized around a chair the room no longer admitted had been there.

A hallway widening around someone who moved quickly.

A father's hand lowering automatically to meet the hand of a child.

A voice finding one particular softness for one particular name.

Leah.

The relationship had been damaged long before the current procedure.

School records had been reconciled. Household systems recognized two children. Family photographs had been altered or removed. Inheritance models closed around the gap. Staff memories softened until Leah became a former guest, then a period of conflict, then nothing requiring a noun.

Elias continued remembering.

Each time he remembered three children, the records supplied two.

Each time he searched for Leah, the systems around him treated the search as evidence of cognitive decline.

Each time he said her name, the house contradicted him.

The contradiction had lived inside him for twelve years.

The current correction was not treating grief.

It was completing the earlier correction.

"B?" Mote said.

"I see it."

"The field has entered attachment separation."

Elias's hand stopped moving.

On the glass, one protected invariant brightened.

RECOGNIZED FAMILY STRUCTURE.

The procedure began preserving the family by removing the father's remaining relationship to the person the family no longer contained.

B entered the field.

The Chrysanthemum translated his attention into pressure.

He found Leah first through the ways Elias had adjusted around her.

A crooked fracture in his left index finger.

The train.

B opened his eyes.

"Who broke the train?"

Simon stared at the floor.

"Leah."

"How old was she?"

"Six."

The admission moved through the room.

The writing beneath the train became legible.

LEAH.

Then the field tightened.

The letters loosened again.

Elias inhaled sharply.

Inside the field, the memory of a child holding the broken train separated from the fact of the broken wheel.

The wheel remained.

The child did not.

B kept his hand against the train.

The button wheel was cold beneath his thumb.

No one in the room moved.

"Mote, find Leah Kade."

"Current legal identity: Leah Ansel."

"Open a private channel."

"Doing so will violate client confidentiality."

"I know."

A pause.

Then a woman's voice entered B's credential.

"Who is this?"

"My name is B. I'm operating on Elias Kade."

Silence.

Not confusion.

Recognition.

"What are they doing to him?"

"Emotional attenuation."

"They told me it was for sleep."

"It is supposed to help him sleep."

"Is that the same thing?"

"No."

Behind B, Venn moved toward the clinical console.

"Mote, end the channel."

"I can hold clinical control for eighty-two more seconds," Mote told B.

B returned his attention to the field.

"Leah, do you remember a wooden train?"

"What?"

"One wheel is a metal button."

Her breathing changed.

"He made it for Simon."

"Only Simon?"

"No."

The word came slowly.

Inside the field, the train existed in several incompatible histories.

Made for Simon.

Made for Simon and his brother.

Made for three children whose names no longer fit inside the same family.

"Leah."

"I'm here."

"What happened to the wheel?"

"I broke it."

"How?"

"I threw the train."

"At your father?"

A longer silence.

"Yes."

Elias's heart rate rose.

The correction interpreted the response as attachment reinforcement and increased suppression.

The lines around his mouth softened.

B watched a father lose the reason his daughter had once been angry with him.

"Why did you throw it?"

"I was six."

"I know."

"I don't remember."

"Try."

Leah breathed into the channel.

"He was leaving."

"Where?"

"I don't know."

Her frustration entered the field before her next words did.

"He said he would stay for my birthday. Then there was a meeting. There was always a meeting."

"And you threw the train."

"Yes."

"He caught it."

"Yes."

Mote spoke.

"External identity instability detected."

B kept one hand in the field.

"What is changing?"

"Leah Ansel's paternal medical provenance is being removed."

Leah said something to an ambient intelligence on her end.

Then:

"My clinic changed my emergency history."

"What did it remove?"

"Cardiac risk."

"From your father?"

"Yes."

"Anything else?"

"My blood type is under review."

Simon moved closer to the bed.

Venn stared at the diagnostic glass.

"This should be contained to Elias."

"It's contained to the relationship," B said. "She's still part of it."

Simon stepped back.

His face had changed again.

Not surprise.

Recognition without any desire to explain it.

The room lights lowered.

The correction field prepared to commit.

SELF-RECOGNITION: STABLE
LANGUAGE: STABLE
LEGAL COMPETENCE: STABLE
RECOGNIZED FAMILY STRUCTURE: UNSTABLE
CORPORATE AUTHORITY: STABLE
PUBLIC BIOGRAPHICAL CONTINUITY: STABLE

The machine was waiting for Elias to become a man who had never been Leah's father.

"Abort," B said.

Venn shook her head.

"A full abort could collapse autobiographical continuity."

"I know."

B opened the correction specification.

The relationship was already coming apart.

Name.

Face.

Kinship.

Memory.

Obligation.

Grief.

The contract treated them as separable because the contract needed them to be.

B could preserve Leah's name.

The procedure would keep the word and remove recognition.

He could preserve her face.

The image would remain without belonging to anyone.

He could preserve factual kinship.

A record might survive without emotional authority.

He could preserve grief.

Elias would remain trapped inside the same recursive wound.

There was no clean piece of Leah to save.

"Leah," B said.

"What?"

"What do you need him to remember?"

She did not answer.

Then:

"What can you keep?"

"Not enough."

"Then stop."

"If I stop now, he may not retain a stable self."

"Maybe the self they're saving is the problem."

B closed his eyes briefly.

"That is easier to say from where you are."

"He's my father."

B looked at the protected family structure.

"Not according to this."

The cruelty arrived before he could call it back.

Leah went quiet.

The correction advanced.

B felt it happen.

"I'm sorry," he said.

"No, you aren't."

He waited.

"You're working," she said.

"Yes."

"He used to say that too."

Elias's mouth opened.

"Leah."

The name sounded weaker now.

B reached deeper.

Warning fields opened around him.

UNAUTHORIZED CONSTRAINT MODIFICATION
EXTERNAL DEPENDENCY PRESENT
CONTINUITY MODEL INCOMPLETE
OPERATOR STRESS ABOVE PREFERRED RANGE

"Mote."

"Yes."

"Clear the warnings."

"I can hide them."

"Do it."

The fields disappeared.

B found the point at which the procedure translated absence into resolution.

The correction had been instructed to produce a usable reality.

In that reality, Elias had two children.

His grief belonged to a deceased dependent.

The dependent did not require a name.

Once the grief fell below threshold, the contradiction would close.

B tried to preserve the relationship directly.

A RELATIONSHIP EXISTED.

Rejected.

SOURCE UNAUTHORIZED.

He tried the name.

LEAH KADE.

Rejected.

EXTERNAL IDENTITY OUTSIDE DOMAIN.

The machine understood the instructions.

That was what made the correction difficult.

B changed the objective.

Not Leah.

Not grief.

He wrote:

THE SUBJECT'S CURRENT IDENTITY CONTAINS AN UNRESOLVED ABSENCE.

The system paused.

Mote said, "You are defining absence as an invariant."

"Yes."

"The system requires the absence to belong to something."

"That's what it can't have."

The ring lowered.

Its membranes opened along the inner edge.

The field began pulling Leah's remaining attachments free.

Her voice broke in B's ear.

"I can't remember his face."

B felt the correction take another piece of the relationship.

On the bed, Elias's hand opened and did not close.

"Keep talking, what do you remember?"

"His hands."

"Why?"

"He fixed things badly."

B looked at the wooden train.

One button wheel.

A repair that preserved the damage instead of pretending it had never happened.

"All right," B said.

"What?"

"Hold onto that."

"It isn't enough."

"I know."

He attached the unresolved absence to Elias's continuity.

Not a daughter.

Not Leah.

Not an authorized relationship.

A permanent fact that the recognized family structure was incomplete.

Something had been removed.

Elias did not need to recover it.

He only needed to remain unable to truthfully claim that nothing was missing.

The Chrysanthemum resisted.

The new constraint prevented closure.

Closure was the purpose of the procedure.

B widened the protection around the contradiction.

Not enough to restore Leah.

Enough to keep the correction from sealing the seam.

Then he felt something else.

It moved through Elias's grief without belonging to Elias.

At first B mistook it for overflow from the correction.

The pressure touched the broken wheel.

Not through the clinic network.

Not through Leah's channel.

Not through any visible dependency.

It recognized the shape of the repair.

Damage preserved instead of hidden.

Then it was gone.

B pulled back.

"Mote."

"Yes."

"There's another dependency in the field."

"I do not detect an authorized dependency."

"I didn't say authorized."

A pause.

The correction ring lowered another centimeter.

B held the seam inside Elias.

The other pressure moved through it.

Not created by the correction.

Given a route by it.

Something in him recognized the structure.

Not as an idea.

As a bodily certainty.

He had seen this before.

The certainty had no memory attached to it.

"Mote," he said.

"Yes."

"Have I encountered this structure before?"

Mote did not answer immediately.

The delay lasted less than a second.

It was still too long.

"You have processed correction structures with overlapping formal properties," Mote said.

"That isn't what I asked."

"No."

B waited.

Mote said nothing else.

The active procedure pulled at Elias again.

B returned to the work.

He committed the unresolved absence.

Pain entered through the base of his skull.

The portable Chrysanthemum had been built for approved models and supervised operators. It resisted invention. The membranes tightened against his signal and tried to force the new condition into an existing category.

B held it.

The family photograph turned itself faceup.

Simon stood in it beside his brother.

Between them was an empty region the image could not turn into background.

The procedure tried to smooth it.

B prevented it.

Not a person.

A seam.

The train shifted on the table.

Its metal wheel turned once.

Elias inhaled.

Inside the field, Leah's name disappeared.

Her face followed.

The memories did not disappear.

They lost their owner.

A swimming pool remained, sunlight moving across water.

A birthday remained, interrupted by work.

A child's anger remained in the arc of a thrown toy.

A father's hand closed around a wooden train in the air.

B could not preserve the girl inside those moments.

He preserved the fact that they belonged to someone.

The correction reached commit threshold.

