Doctor Venn went to Elias.

She checked his pupils, breathing, language response, and identity markers.

"Mr. Kade?"

His eyes opened.

"Can you tell me your name?"

"Elias Kade."

"Where are you?"

"A clinic."

"How many children do you have?"

Simon watched him.

Elias looked toward the ceiling.

"Two."

Simon exhaled.

Then Elias frowned.

"No."

Venn's hands stopped.

Elias looked around the room.

"Two," he said again. "But that isn't all."

Simon's relief disappeared.

"What do you mean?"

"I don't know."

Elias sat up slowly.

His gaze moved across the continuity objects.

The watch.

The bird.

The napkin.

The book.

The photograph.

The train.

He picked up the train and turned it over.

The marks beneath it did not form a readable name.

His thumb rested against the metal wheel.

"Who fixed this?"

Simon said nothing.

Elias looked at him.

"There should be another answer."

Venn turned toward B.

"What did you change?"

"I left the contradiction open."

"It couldn't resolve it without lying."

"Neither was the living daughter."

Simon stepped between them.

"You were supposed to help him."

B looked at Elias.

"He should sleep."

"You don't know that."

"The field stabilized."

"The field said this would be gentle."

Elias held the train in both hands.

He did not remember the person attached to it.

He also could not accept that no person had ever been there.

